{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If his offering is a burnt-offering of the herd, a male without blemish he shall offer it.</b> The reason that the עֹלָה comes as a male, and the חַטָּאת as a female, and the שְׁלָמִים as either male or female, is that the עֹלָה comes for what is upon your spirit — that is, for the sin of thought, as it says (Job 1:5): ״וְהֶעֱלָה עוֹלוֹת מִסְפַּר כֻּלָּם כִּי אָמַר אוּלַי חָטְאוּ בָנַי וּבֵרְכוּ אֱלֹהִים בִּלְבָבָם״. And the חַטָּאת comes for the sin of deed. It follows that one who entertains a sinful thought and it did not issue into action is surely a mighty one on earth, for as his strength is, so is his conquest over his impulse — since the thought did not go forth outward to do a strange deed; therefore his offering is a male. But one who sins also in deed — his strength has weakened like a female's, for he was unable to stand against his impulse; therefore his offering is a female in cases like this. And since in any case both the עֹלָה and the חַטָּאת come for sin whose source is from the heart, as it says ״לֵב הוּתַל הִטָּהוּ״ (Isaiah 44:20), and the wicked are under the dominion of their heart, whose place is on the left side — therefore their slaughter is in the north. But the שְׁלָמִים, which run without transgression — their slaughter is in any place in the courtyard, and there is no distinction for them between male and female." "And further: the עֹלָה comes for the sin of thought, which hangs upon the faculty of intellect in man, which is likened to a male, for the intellect is the principal of the house and its ruler. Therefore its offering is a male, and the עֹלָה is entirely for the Most High — corresponding to the spirit of the children of man, which rises upward; so too the עֹלָה upon its pyre rises aloft, for the nature of fire as well is to rise upward." "But the חַטָּאת comes for the sin of deed, which is performed by means of the instruments of action that depend on matter, which is receptive like a female; therefore its offering is a female. And the essence of sin depends on designated organs, foremost among them fat and blood, and through them comes every strife and every affliction — for through the boiling of the blood, all day long he craves a craving for forbidden things, as it is written (Deuteronomy 12:20): ״כִּי תְאַוֶּה נַפְשְׁךָ״, and it is written (ibid. 12:23): ״כִּי הַדָּם הוּא הַנָּפֶשׁ״. And likewise when Jeshurun grows fat and goes awry from the fat of his eyes (Psalms 73:7) and ״חֶלְבָּמוֹ סָגְרוּ פִּימוֹ״ (ibid. 17:10), then he is predisposed all the more toward sin. Therefore from the חַטָּאת one offers the fat and the blood, and the flesh is eaten by the priests, as an act of righteousness to feed the poor, the ministers of our Elohim — for by kindness and truth is iniquity atoned." "And the שְׁלָמִים make peace in the world, for part of it goes to the altar, part to the priests, and part to the owners, and all of them eat at one table, and this demonstrates the peace among them all, for there is in it no aspect of sin that causes strife between HaShem and His people. And there is a distinction between חַטָּאת and עֹלָה: the חַטָּאת comes only for the inadvertent, that is, what a person does without willing it, whereas the עֹלָה atones for deliberate thought, that is, with willing. By way of example: one who entertains thoughts about a woman and knows it is forbidden to think of her, but his heart compelled him. Therefore it says ״יַקְרִיב אֹתוֹ לִרְצוֹנוֹ לִפְנֵי HaShem״ — that it atones even upon the will, as said above. And from the extra word ״אוֹתוֹ״, some explain that Scripture accounts it for him as though he offered himself before HaShem."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:12.663625436Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}